pend $2,000 that you  really can't afford you should have to  pay for that you buy a house with five  bedrooms and a spa bath that you really  can't afford you really should have to  pay for that or lose that house but  ordinary people who've done ordinary  transactions deserve just some ordinary  instruments to be able to do that if  that's the case that will not only help  us at the family level it will  completely reform what our financial  institutions look like and ultimately  give us a far more secure financial  system so in the short run they Bank  then who has been making their income  off of taking advantage of families they  may hurt in the in the short run but in  the long run we as a country our economy  and our whole credit markets will come  out better  absolutely congressman Schneider I can't  say it better than that because that is  the answer thank you thank you thank you  madam chairman thank you for holding  this hearing today thank you for your  leadership and thank you is mourn for  what you're doing I've always believed  that we need to restore stability in our  financial markets and you've been a  leader with that and to do that we have  to get the best possible results for the  American taxpayers at the lowest cost  and to me this means transparency and  accountability and that is your focus  and I believe when you look at the past  previous to this administration there  was not that kind of accountability and  transparency we did not have that and  while there's there's  criticisms we can lob now and we're in a  transition period when you look at what  got us into this mess in the first place  loopholes that were allowed to be opened  and there's blame on both sides for that  the the leverage that was allowed to the  SEC allowed back in they was Oh for and  just a bunch of things that went wrong  from Bernie Madoff on that shouldn't  have happened and so that's why I  appreciate what you're trying to do here  and in a way you're starting fro