rt a  headed poor information puts us in a  position of trading in in scale rumors  so I'm a strong believer that the more  information we can get out there and the  more reliable that information is you  know there'll be some bumps at the  beginning but not only will there be  readjustment we begin to rebuild on a  solid foundation  so my view and I believe our panel's  view on stress tests unanimously here is  yes we like them we think they could be  stronger and we think they should be  used in more ways and more often okay  one of the other things that you've  talked about before is this idea of  systemic risks how a few actors can do  some bad things and then suddenly the  whole system starts going under so we  are looking right now at reforms for  regulating the the market and we want to  do it in a prudent way and we want to do  it quickly what do you think we should  be doing well I'm gonna I'm gonna start  with how to frame the issue because  that's the one that concerns me the most  the the really tricky part in this one  is to identify what it is that puts us  at risk is it sighs is it concentration  is it the kind of industry you work in  you know we've had big companies fail  and long failed it was it was huge  Worldcon failed it was huge and those  businesses were liquidated and they were  liquidated a very short period of time  very serious consequences for example in  the case of Enron employees who were  laid off and I'm not saying it's not  done without pain but I don't know if  you know this and Ron was making the  argument shortly before it was forced  into bankruptcy that it would cost the  systemic failure that it was out there  in the power industry that it was such  an important player in that industry and  of course that wasn't true at all we  said this is the problem this is where  you are financially or  to liquidate so what I want to emphasize  here is the importance as we look at it  about identifying what the risk is  before we just hea